{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: repeated unvalidated submissions after one demo

In this instance, the only demo had a 29×11 input and a 14×11 output, while the test was 4×9. The demo showed a dense structured field, a full-width separator row (color 4), and a uniform region below it; the output preserved the top 14×11 portion while changing its color-1 cells to color 2. This was strong evidence that the task was not a simple mapping of the test's visible 4×4 color-3 motif to a color-2 motif. The test also contained a full-height color-1 divider and color-3 regions, so its partition/representation needed to be inferred via a discriminating additional demo.

The unsuccessful actions repeatedly submitted variants of the same guessed 4×4 output, including an identical grid more than once, and claimed an overlap rule without demo support. After a wrong submission, reopen task class and output dimensions; do not repeat an identical grid or cosmetic variant. If the one demo does not establish how a differently sized test is represented, spend a demo request to resolve the mapping rather than burn attempts on unsupported guesses. Keep claims about the rule out of the response unless evidence validates them. In puzzle mode, still emit exactly one permitted JSON action on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md", "content": "# Compact output representation and task-f0caf098 review

The demos repeatedly presented large regular lattices but returned compact outputs whose dimensions varied substantially. A key caution is that identifying separator crossings or extracting marker colors is only a candidate representation: neither the test's lattice dimensions nor a plausible crossing matrix establishes the output shape/content. Across demonstrations, explicitly align region/intersection positions to each compact output and test the precise encoding (including blank/default cells, omitted borders, ordering, and exceptional marker values). Require exact replay of every demo before submission.

In the reviewed instance, I made successive unsupported compact-grid guesses (including 4x4 and 5x5 crossing matrices) despite failed feedback. After any incorrect compact-output submission, restart representation inference from the demos and record which assumption each failure rejects; do not repeat the same candidate or cosmetically alter its entries. The available skill references already flag this output-dimension/mapping issue; this note captures the concrete failure pattern and emphasizes full representation validation before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/failed-guess-reassessment.md", "content": "# Failed-submission reassessment

When a plausible full-grid cleanup or marker-repair guess is incorrect, treat that as evidence against the whole inferred rule, not a cue to make another nearby edit. Reconstruct the exact input/output delta in every demo, including unchanged markers and structures. Separate the observed operation from assumptions about color regions and object boundaries. If multiple rules still fit, name them and request a demo specifically predicted to differ; otherwise submit only a transformation replayed against all available examples. This avoids cascading unsupported guesses and needless penalties.
